  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nWe are to find a step function h(x) on the interval [0,2] that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted version, with the constraint that the integral of h is 1. This problem is highly non-convex and requires an innovative strategy.\n\nOur approach combines a multi-stage optimization procedure with a novel careful refinement using basis function expansions and cross-resolution projection. Unlike the previous methods that either overrely on Bayesian optimization or parameterization of basis functions, we introduce a:\n- Three-stage optimization framework with explicit hyperparameter tuning\n- Basis function selection mechanism inspired by wavelet thresholding\n- Resolution-mapping correlation evaluation to ensure objective consistency\n- Heuristic adaptive projection that maintains solution viability across scales\n\nThe core idea is to forward-propagate information through scales while systematically pruning dominated solutions. This is motivated by the observation that theoretical bounds often arise from interactions between scale-dependent clipping operations and the underlying smoothness of optimal solutions.\n\nThe key limitation is that this represents an approximate heuristic solution, but the methods are designed to scale to industrial-grade computation within the given budget.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nGiven inputs:\n- A base function h\u2080: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] (from initial_h_values)\n- Objective to minimize: C\u2085(h) = max\u2096 \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nWe parameterize h\u2098(x) (mapping to resolution m) as:\nh\u2098(x) = \u2211\u1d62\u1d62(l) * \u03c6\u1d62(x, \u03c3\u1d62)\n\nWhere:\n- \u03c6\u1d62 are localized amplitude-constrained Gaussian functions\n- l\u1d62 are normalized scale-free coefficients\n- \u03c3\u1d62 are support widths\n- m is the resolution parameter\n\nConstraints:\n- \u2211\u1d62 l\u1d62 = 1 (integral normalization)\n- 0 \u2264 l\u1d62 \u2264 1\n- \u03c3\u1d62 \u2208 [0.05, 0.5] \\ e^(-mapping of log-scale)\n\nThe optimization computes:\nC\u2085(h\u2098) = dx\u2098 * max\u2096 (h\u2098(x)(1-h\u2098(x+k)))\n\nWhere dx\u2098 = 2/m\n\nThe core acceptance criterion is:\nnew_candidate \u227a best_candidate if C\u2085(new_candidate) < C\u2085(best_candidate) OR\n(C\u2085(new_candidate) \u2248 C\u2085(best_candidate) and candidate satisfies higher-order smoothness criteria)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n3.1 Dadaptation and data preprocessing\n- Input validation: Check input h_values for condition (h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h)\u22481)\n- Resolution mapping: \n  l\u5efa\u7acb\u8d77: m_base = len(initial_h_values), other_m = sorted(set([m \u2208 {200, 300, 400, 500}}))\n  project_feasible(parent_vector, n/2)\n- Precompute basis for all resolutions: Gaussian members of size M_basis (M_basis = 25)\n\n3.2 Multi-stage optimization loop\nPhase 1: (50% budget time):\n  For m \u2208 [other_m] in increasing order:\n    Subset = sample basis functions (size = min(5*m^{1/2}, 60))\n    Initialize population = [rand_proj(parent_vector, shift=\u03b4) for \u03b4 \u2208 [-0.5,0.5]]\n    Run Bayesian optimization with:\n      Surrogate model (Gaussian Process)\n      Acquisition function: Expected Improvement\n      Dimensions = len(basis_functions)\n      Constraints: Sum-normalized and bounds [0,1]\n\nPhase 2: (30% budget):\n  For top solutions from phase1:\n    Coarsen basis selection and apply resolution-adaptive projection (map solution to lower resolution)\n    Enforce thriftiness: discard solutions that don't improve noise-filtered C\u2085 value\n    Run Nelder-Mead optimization with surrogate constraints\n\nPhase 3: (20% budget):\n  Multi-population selection: meet selection conditions\n    Apply random pitch-translation perturbation (stochastic perturbation)\n    Restructure function to use sparse essence (threshold l\u1d62 below 1e-5)\n  Return firefly-inspired pairing between top performers from different phases\n\n3.3 Algorithm steps\n- List_operations(h, resolution) -> interpolate/extrapolate to given resolution\n- fa function C\u2085(h) implementation via:\n  1. np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n  2. element-wise operations on correlation\n  3. dx application\n- rkhp_project_feasible(h) -> enforce integral and bounds\n- monte_carlo_bound_check(h) -> validate C\u2085 with randomized sampling\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\n4.1 Basis configuration (always active)\n- Basis_size = {200: 64, 300: 72, 400: 80, 500: 96}\n- Gaussian with adaptive width: \u03c3 = 0.4 - 0.1*m^{-0.2}\n- Orthogonalization condition: Gram-Schmidt variant with bounded inner products\n\n4.2 Acquisition/selection adjustments (conditional on performance)\n4.2.1 Epsilon-greedy selection (active)\n  Probability = 0.7 for continuing with best, 0.3 for interleaving recent contenders (avoiding myopic convergence)\n\n4.2.2 Dimensionality reduction (adaptive)\n  If dimension 2DEx4?): employ PCA to reduce components, set eigenvalue threshold at \u03bb > 0.01 * max(eigenvalues)\n\n4.2.3 Noise tolerance (active)\n  Use median-filtered correlation values during acquisition function evaluation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n5.1 Resource estimation\n- Primary task: basis = 50 Gaussian functions \u2192 450 decision variables, each \u2248 100ms evaluation \u2192 45 seconds\n- Bayesian optimization surplus scaling: 32 high-cost evaluations \u2192 32 seconds\n- Total phase1 \u2248 110 seconds budget time\n- Monitoring point: after 70% of phase1, evaluate L2 condition number of covariance matrix\n\n5.2 Failure recovery\n- Missing initial points: seed from list_operations(parent, m)\n- Projection failure: implement back-up piecewise-linear interpolation\n- Timeslice overflow: abort and fall back to projected-rnd result\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n6.1 Feasibility verification\n- Compute list_operations(best, 2) and project_feasible\n- Final_che - ck C\u2085 implementation consistency (must match parent gauge)\n- If C\u2085 is above threshold (0.3808), sample at three resolutions for statistical diversity\n\n6.2 Return contract\n- {h_final, C\u2085(h_final), resolution_used}\n  Where h_final: an array of length=resolution_used\n  C\u2085(h_final): float\n\n6.3 Runtime constraint\n- Maximum wall-time per resolution: ceil(0.9/length_multiplier)*base_time(expressed in fractions of initial budget)\n- Monitor accumulate best solution every 10 evaluations\n\n7. Theoretical convergence\n- We appeal to concentration bounds: a-Greedy solution matches /error\u22641 if basis is large enough (>40), variations in \u03c3 account for oscillations.",
